  "experiment_id": "plaque-day",
  "description": "A day of plaque, from 07:00: 60 um of plaque under the film, the mouth and the air of the oxygen profile scene. Breakfast at 07:15, lunch at 12:30 and dinner at 19:00 are chewed, each releasing 15 mmol of sugar into the mouth; a sweet is sucked at 10:30 and another at 15:30, then sugar-free gum chewed for 20 minutes. The teeth are brushed at 07:45 and 22:00, each brushing taking 42% of the plaque off from its surface down (Slot et al. 2012), and the tongue and cheeks wear its surface at 0.5 um per h. Chewing adds 1 mL per min to the flow. The plaque grows on sugar and on saliva up to 150 um, and the day brings it back close to where it started. The habits, the wear and the chewing flow are illustrative (confidence C). Otherwise the network and the mouth of the Stephan curve scene (stephan_rinse.json), whose description gives their sources. The film's surface is at the air, which holds oxygen at 0.21 mM, its solubility at 37 C (Benson and Krause 1984), and the mouth's saliva at saturation too. Saliva carries glycoproteins: its mucins MG1 and MG2 come to about 0.37 g per L (Payment et al. 2000), 68 to 78% carbohydrate (Levine et al. 1987), about 1.5 mM as hexose, of which the scenes take 1 mM as available. The plaque grows on it where oxygen reaches, at up to 0.06 per h with a yield of 3 C-mol per hexose, respires a little of it for maintenance, at 0.0015 per h, and respires sugar too, at 0.0035 per h; growth on sugar, at up to 0.3 per h, makes lactic acid as it does. New biomass brings its share of the carboxyl groups that buffer it, and packs the column from the substratum up. Oxygen diffuses at 1.13e-9 m2 per s, 0.43 of its rate in water at 37 C, the mean for small solutes in biofilms (Stewart 1998). The three respiration rates are calibrated (confidence C) so that oxygen reaches as deep into thick plaque as von Ohle et al. (2010) measured. docs/parameters.md grades every value.",
